/**
\page org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2 The Volume Visualization v2 View

\imageMacro{volume_visualization_v2-dox.svg,"Icon of the Volume Visualization v2 View.",2.00}

\tableofcontents

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_overview Overview

Volume visualization draws a 3D image as a solid body in the 3D render window.
Instead of showing one slice at a time, it sends a ray through the volume for
every pixel and accumulates colour and opacity from every voxel that ray meets.
Bone, vessels or skin can therefore be seen at once, in depth, without cutting
the data into planes first.

What decides whether you see bone, skin or fog is the <b>transfer function</b>:
curves that map each grey value to a colour and to an opacity. Almost everything
in this view is either a way of choosing a transfer function or a rendering
option applied on top of one.

\imageMacro{QmitkVolumeVisualizationV2_Overview.png,"An abdominal CT rendered as a volume with the CT-AAA preset.",7.00}

\note Volume rendering is done on the graphics card and has no software
fallback. On a machine whose GPU cannot run the ray caster, the 3D window stays
empty and the preset previews described below are not drawn either. Volume
rendering also needs a large amount of memory; very large images may not fit.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_image Image

The selector at the top lists the 3D and 4D images in the Data Manager. Until
one is picked the rest of the view is not shown at all, since every control in
it acts on an image. Pick one and press <b>Enable volume rendering</b>; the
button names what pressing it does, so it reads <b>Disable volume rendering</b>
once the volume is on. Nothing below the selector responds while it is off,
because there is no volume to configure yet.

Switching it on does more than show the volume: a node that carries no transfer
function is given one from the preset catalogue, and one that names no lighting
model is given the one the 3D window is lit for, as described in
\ref org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_lighting. Switching on therefore
produces a visible volume rather than an empty 3D window, and you adjust from
there.

Which preset that is follows from the image. One whose DICOM metadata reports MR
starts from <tt>MR-Default</tt>; everything else starts from <tt>CT-AAA</tt>,
including an image carrying no such metadata - a NIfTI or an NRRD from outside
MITK usually carries none. Seeing a CT preset on an MR loaded from one of those
formats is therefore expected; pick an <tt>MR-</tt> preset from the grid.

For a 4D image the volume follows the current time step, so stepping through
time in the image navigator steps the volume with it.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf Transfer function

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_presets Presets

The presets are laid out as a grid, each one shown as a small rendering of the
image you currently have selected. The preview is what applying that preset will
look like on your data, not a generic illustration, which is why the presets are
chosen by picture rather than by name. They are drawn one after another, so they
appear progressively.

\imageMacro{QmitkVolumeVisualizationV2_Presets.png,"Each preset is shown as a rendering of the selected image.",12.00}

The button above the grid, at its right, lays the same presets out as a list
instead: one row each, the name beside a preview too small to study but
large enough to say which colours a preset brings. More of the catalogue is on
screen that way, and the names line up in one column, which is the quicker way
in once they are familiar. Pressing it again brings the grid back; nothing is
drawn a second time either way. The view starts in the grid every time it is
opened.

The catalogue is the Hounsfield-based preset library shared across medical
viewers, plus a few general-purpose colour maps. The <tt>CT-</tt> presets assume
Hounsfield units, so which tissue a preset picks out transfers between CT images
unchanged. How solid that tissue looks does not: opacity accumulates over the
distance a ray travels in millimetres, so the same preset comes out denser on a
coarsely sampled image than on a finely sampled one. <b>Height</b>, under
<b>Opacity curve</b>, is the slider that compensates. The <tt>MR-</tt> presets
cannot assume even the units, because MR intensities are not calibrated to a
fixed scale, so expect to adjust those per image.

\note Almost every preset uses the <b>Composite</b> blend mode, which builds a
three-dimensional image in which nearer tissue hides what lies behind it.
<tt>CT-MIP</tt> and <tt>MR-MIP</tt> are the exceptions: they render a maximum
intensity projection, which keeps the brightest value along each viewing ray.
Depth is lost, but dense structures such as contrast-filled vessels stay visible
however much tissue surrounds them. The mode comes with the preset, can be
changed while editing the curve, and while it is anything other than Composite,
a line under the rendering button names it.

The preset in force is the entry marked in the grid. An asterisk after its name
says the curve has since been moved away from it, whether by the sliders
described below or by editing it point by point; clicking that entry again
rebuilds the preset as its author wrote it. Only a curve that no preset
describes at all - one whose preset was removed while in use - leaves no entry
marked.

Right-clicking the grid offers <b>Save as preset...</b> for the curve you have
arrived at. It is available exactly while the asterisk is showing and greyed out
otherwise, since a curve that is still its preset is one the catalogue already
holds. Choose where to write it and it joins the end of the grid as an entry of
its own, with its own preview, marked and no longer starred: it is a preset now
rather than a departure from one.

A saved preset is remembered by the file it was written to rather than copied
anywhere, so it is in the grid again at the next start, and editing that file
changes what its entry brings. One whose file has since been moved or deleted is
dropped from the grid, with the reason written to the log, and is not looked for
again.

The last entry of the grid, <b>Load preset...</b>, adds a preset from a JSON
file written earlier - here, or on another machine and shared. The file joins
the grid and is remembered exactly like a preset saved here, and is applied
straight away. Choosing a file the grid already offers applies its entry rather
than adding it a second time.

Right-clicking a preset of your own offers <b>Remove preset</b> for the entry
under the cursor, after asking. That entry leaves the grid and is not looked for
again at the next start, while the file it was saved to stays where you put it -
a preset is that file rather than a copy of it, and the <b>Load preset...</b>
entry brings it back. The menu entry is greyed out over the catalogue's own presets,
which are read afresh at every start and would be offered again regardless.

Removing the preset a volume is currently rendered with leaves the rendering
untouched. Its curve stays exactly as it is and becomes one that answers to no
preset, so no entry is marked for it any more.

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_canvas Reading the curve

Below the preset grid, one canvas shows three things at once: the histogram of
the image as a backdrop, the opacity curve drawn over it, and the colours the
transfer function assigns as a gradient beneath. Together they say which grey
values the current function makes visible and in what colour, against how much
of that value actually occurs in the image.

Until you ask to edit it, the canvas is a display. Clicking it does nothing; the
curve is moved as a whole with the sliders described next. The <b>Edit</b>
button above it, at its right, turns the same canvas into a point-by-point
editor, described in \ref org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_custom.

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_adjust Adjusting a preset

A preset lands where its author put it, which is rarely exactly where your image
wants it. Below the canvas, four sliders move it without redrawing it. Two of
them, under <b>Opacity curve</b>, act on how much is visible:

<ul>
<li> <b>Position</b> slides the opacity curve along the grey-value axis, which
is what you reach for when a preset shows too much or too little tissue. It
travels half the image's value range in either direction.
<li> <b>Height</b> raises or lowers the whole curve, making the volume more or
less solid without changing which values are visible at all.
</ul>

The other two, under <b>Color</b>, act on the range those colours are spread
over:

<ul>
<li> <b>Position</b> moves the centre of that range.
<li> <b>Width</b> sets how wide it is. Narrowing it concentrates the colours
into a smaller band of grey values; widening it spreads them out.
</ul>

<b>Reset</b> returns all four to neutral. That restores the curve they are
measured from, which is the untouched preset, the curve as you left it after
editing it point by point, or the curve on show when its preset was removed. It
does not reload a preset; clicking the preset's entry does.

What is remembered is the preset together with the four offsets, rather than
only the resulting curve, so selecting the image again later brings the sliders
back to the positions you left them in.

\subsection org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_tf_custom Editing the curve point by point

<b>Edit</b>, above the canvas, hands that canvas over to editing the curve on
show point by point; pressing it again ends the edit. The preset grid and the
four sliders are greyed out in the meantime, since either would replace what you
are drawing.

\imageMacro{QmitkVolumeVisualizationV2_EditMode.png,"The canvas while editing.",10.00}

<ol>
<li> <b>Edit</b> starts and ends the edit.
<li> <b>Fit to curve</b> decides how far the axis reaches, as described below.
<li> The handles of the opacity curve.
<li> The colour stops, as markers along the bottom edge. A faded one standing on
an edge lies beyond the axis.
<li> The <b>Color stops</b> row, for the stop selected on the canvas.
</ol>

The canvas edits both curves at once, each in a region of its own:

<ul>
<li> In the plot, the handles are the points of the opacity curve. Left-click
adds a point, dragging moves one, and right-click removes it. The arrow keys
move the selected point and DELETE removes it.
<li> Along the bottom edge, below the plot, the markers are the colour stops,
each pointing at the grey value its colour applies to. Click one to select it,
drag it to move it, double-click it to pick its colour, and right-click it to
remove it. Clicking the strip between markers adds a stop there, taking the
colour the gradient already has at that point, so that adding one changes
nothing until you recolour it.
<li> Between points the curves are interpolated linearly.
</ul>

A row under the canvas, <b>Color stops</b>, reaches the same stops without
aiming at a marker. Its list shows each stop as a swatch, in the order the
markers stand in. The colour field beside it is the selected stop's colour and
opens a colour picker. The number gives the stop's position as a fraction of the
axis, 0 at its left end and 1 at its right; typing a value moves the stop. The
<b>+</b> button adds a stop in the middle of the widest gap, and <b>-</b>
removes the selected one. A gradient keeps at least one stop, and the curve at
least one point.

The axis covers the band of grey values the image occupies, while the presets
are authored on their modality's full scale, so a preset's outermost stops
often lie beyond either end of the axis. The one nearest each end is then drawn
faded on that end, to say it exists rather than where it is. It can be selected
and recoloured there, but not dragged, since there is no place on the axis to
drag it from. Clearing <b>Fit to curve</b>, beside <b>Edit</b>, widens the axis
to take in every point of both curves, so that each stop stands at its own value
and can be dragged like any other. The image's band then takes up only part of
the plot. Every edit starts with the box ticked, and the axis returns to the
band when the edit ends.

The list beside <b>Edit</b>, shown only while editing, is the blend mode the
curve is rendered in. It starts on the mode the preset brought. Changing it
counts as an edit like any drawn on the canvas: the preset's entry is starred,
and the choice lasts as long as the drawing does, so <b>Save as preset...</b>
is how to keep it. Only <b>Composite</b> builds a three-dimensional image and
takes lighting; the others flatten each ray to a single value.

Ending the edit keeps whatever you drew; there is no way back to the curve that
stood before it other than clicking the preset's entry, which rebuilds the
preset. If you changed nothing, the preset and the slider positions are left as
they were. If you did, the preset's entry is starred, and the drawing becomes
what the sliders are measured from: they return to neutral and from then on move
the drawing rather than the preset.

To keep a curve you have drawn beyond the session, right-click the preset grid
and choose <b>Save as preset...</b>: it writes a JSON file in the same format
the built-in catalogue uses, so your own functions can be kept and shared and
added back with the <b>Load preset...</b> entry.

\note A curve edited point by point is not kept. What the view records is the
preset it was drawn over together with the slider positions, so selecting
another image and coming back rebuilds those and the drawing is gone; the
asterisk says the curve is no longer the preset while it is still on screen, and
<b>Save as preset...</b> is how to keep one. Slider positions are kept, since
they are part of that record rather than a departure from it. A saved file
records the blend mode the curve was authored for alongside it, and loading it
restores both.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_lighting Lighting and shading

Shading gives the volume the shape cues a flat colour cannot: where a surface
turns away from the light, where two structures touch, how deep a recess goes.

It is always on for a volume rendered here. How the volume is lit is chosen on
the 3D render window itself, from the lighting button in its top right corner.
The lights belong to the window rather than to any one volume, so the choice
applies to every volume drawn there, including one hidden at the time, and it
stays as you left it after this view is closed.

Default lighting is where every 3D window starts, so a window on it is taken to
be one nobody has set: there the first lit volume brings back the lighting it
was last shown with, which is how a saved scene reopens lit as it was saved. A
window lit that way returns to the lighting chosen in its menu once no volume in
it is lit any more - switched off, hidden, or on a projection blend mode.

Volumes only catch up with the window while this view is open. A choice made in
the menu while it is closed changes the lights straight away, but the volumes
keep the settings tuned for the previous lighting, and may look different or
render more slowly, until the view is opened again. Hiding or showing a volume
in the Data Manager is not noticed by this view either: the lighting follows at
the next change made here, such as selecting another image.

\imageMacro{QmitkVolumeVisualizationV2_LightingMenu.png,"The lighting menu of the 3D render window.",5.00}

The three models are not weaker and stronger versions of the same effect - they
take different paths through the renderer:

<ul>
<li> <b>Default lighting</b> is the renderer's own five-light kit, which a 3D
window uses until something asks for anything else. It is the flattest of the
three and the most expensive to shade a volume with, and is offered so that a
window can be put back the way it was.
<li> <b>Headlight</b> puts a single light at the camera. Nothing casts a
visible shadow, because the light reaches exactly what you can see. In exchange
it is the only model in which the <b>Ambient</b> slider carries the tissue's own
colour, so ambient light fills the dark areas instead of laying flat grey over
the picture. A volume switched on in a window still on Default lighting starts
here; one switched on in a window already set to another model takes that model
instead.
<li> <b>Key light</b> puts the main light off to one side and adds a dimmer one
opposite it, then traces short secondary rays through the volume so that
contacts and recesses darken. The rays are kept short deliberately: long ones
let one structure cast a shadow onto another, which describes the lighting
rather than the anatomy. This model costs more per still frame, switches its
shadows off while you rotate, and also relights any surface sharing the 3D
window.
</ul>

<b>Ambient</b>, <b>Diffuse</b>, <b>Specular</b> and <b>Specular power</b>
control how one volume's material responds to that light, and stay in this
panel because they are per volume rather than per window. Choosing a model sets
all four to a sensible starting point; moving them afterwards does not leave the
model, and <b>Reset</b> puts them back to the values the current model
prescribes. Ambient is deliberately zero under Key light: with more than one
light in the scene the renderer adds ambient as a flat grey wash, so the fill
light does that job instead.

\note This whole section applies to the Composite blend mode only. With
<tt>CT-MIP</tt> or <tt>MR-MIP</tt> it is disabled, with the reason on its
header: a maximum intensity projection reduces each ray to a single value and
lights nothing, so lighting there is inert rather than merely subtle.

\section org_mitk_views_volumevisualization_v2_appearance Colour and opacity from the Data Manager

A volume's appearance comes from its transfer function, not from the node's
generic appearance properties.

The Data Manager's Opacity slider fades the image in the 2D windows, and in the
3D window it fades the slice planes the image is textured onto - but not the
volume itself. Use the opacity part of the transfer function for that.

The Color action likewise has no effect on a grey image, whose colour the
transfer function supplies. On a binary image it does apply: there the volume is
drawn in that single colour, and the transfer function controls are disabled,
as the view says when you select such an image.
*/
